# `{{RECORDS_DIR}}/language.md` — the words this project uses

> Copy this to `{{RECORDS_DIR}}/language.md` and fill it in from the code. **Versioned**, like
> `info.md`: the words are the project's, not one person's.
>
> Every agent reads it at the start of a session. It is short on purpose — a glossary nobody can hold
> in their head is a glossary nobody uses.

## Why this file exists

An agent dropped into a repository has to describe things it has no name for, so it spends twenty
words where one would do, and it spends them **again in the next session**. Worse, it invents its own
names as it goes, and the third one is different from the first two.

One shared word fixes all of that at once:

> **before** — "there is a problem when a lesson inside a section of a course is given a real place
> in the file system"
> **after** — "there is a problem in the materialization cascade"

The payoff compounds past concision: variables, functions and files get named consistently, the
codebase becomes navigable by the same words the humans use, and the agent spends less of its
thinking on translation.

🔴 **This is a record of the words the project already uses, not a place to invent better ones.** A
term you coined here that appears nowhere in the code is a term the next agent will use and nobody
will recognise. If the code and this file disagree, the code wins and this file gets corrected.

## Terms

> One entry per term that a newcomer would get wrong. Not one per class. The test is whether two
> people have already meant different things by it.

**\<Term\>**
: What it is, in one or two sentences, naming the `file:line` where it lives if it has one.
  *Avoid:* the other words people have used for this, so they stop being used.

**\<Term\>**
