## Relationships

> The sentences that say how the terms connect. This is what stops an agent inventing a hierarchy
> the code does not have.

- A **\<Term\>** holds many **\<Term\>**
- A **\<Term\>** has exactly one **\<Term\>** at a time

## Ambiguities that were resolved

> The most valuable section, and the one that decays if you skip it. When one word was being used for
> two things, write which meaning won and which is now retired — otherwise the retired one comes back
> in six months wearing the same spelling.

- "\<word\>" meant both \<A\> and \<B\>. **Resolved:** \<A\> is now **\<Term\>**; \<B\> is
  **\<Term\>**; the bare word is no longer used.

## Words that are NOT domain terms

> Frequently-used words that carry no special meaning here, listed so nobody looks for one.

- \<word\>, \<word\> — ordinary English, no project meaning.
